## Thread.Abort → Cooperative Cancellation Migration

### Pattern Classification

Your code contains **four** patterns that need migration:

| Location | Pattern | Replacement |
|----------|---------|-------------|
| `ProcessLoop` while loop | Cancellable work loop | `CancellationToken` in loop condition |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Exception as control flow | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Reset to keep thread alive | `token.IsCancellationRequested` check |
| `Stop()` → `Thread.Abort()` | Force termination | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` |

### Why This Breaks on .NET 8

`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` — the runtime **cannot** forcibly terminate a thread. Your `Stop()` method will crash, and `ThreadAbortException` will never be thrown, making that catch block dead code.

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token to unblock
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Cancellation requested — clean up partial work
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                // No ResetAbort needed — just continue to finally and exit loop
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null) return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Wait with a timeout instead of Thread.Abort as fallback
        _workerThread?.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

### Critical Pitfalls

**1. `GetNextItem()` must accept and respect the token.**
If it blocks on a `Queue`, `Socket`, or `ManualResetEvent`, cancellation won't interrupt it without changes:

```csharp
// ❌ Blocks forever — cancellation can't interrupt this
private Item GetNextItem() => _queue.Take();

// ✅ Throws OperationCanceledException when token is cancelled
private Item GetNextItem(CancellationToken token) => _queue.Take(token);

// ✅ For WaitHandle-based blocking:
WaitHandle.WaitAny(new[] { _dataReady, token.WaitHandle });
```

**2. Don't replace `Thread.Sleep` with itself.**

```csharp
// ❌ Won't wake on cancellation
Thread.Sleep(2000);

// ✅ Wakes immediately when cancelled
token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(2000);
```

**3. Dispose `CancellationTokenSource`.** It's `IDisposable` — leaking it causes timer/callback leaks. The `IDisposable` pattern on the class handles this.

**4. Cancellation is not immediate.** It only takes effect at the next `IsCancellationRequested` check or token-aware API call. If `ProcessItem` does heavy work, add checkpoints:

```csharp
private void ProcessItem(Item item, CancellationToken token)
{
    StepOne(item);
    token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // checkpoint
    StepTwo(item);
    token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // checkpoint
    StepThree(item);
}
```

**5. The `when` filter matters.** `catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)` avoids catching unrelated cancellations from other tokens.

### Key Behavioral Difference

| Aspect | .NET Framework (old) | .NET 8 (new) |
|--------|---------------------|--------------|
| Stop mechanism | Runtime injects exception anywhere | Code checks token at explicit points |
| Blocked thread | Abort interrupts mid-sleep/wait | Only token-aware waits are interrupted |
| Cleanup guarantee | `ResetAbort` + `finally` | Natural flow — no special API needed |
| Latency to stop | Nearly instant | Depends on checkpoint frequency |
